window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert12-bio-human-repro","kind":"practice","exam":"NEET","year":null,"title":"Human Reproduction · Practice","chapter":"Human Reproduction","subject":"Biology","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-001","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":1,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"The menstrual phase of the human menstrual cycle typically lasts for how long, and what causes the menstrual flow?","image":"","options":["3–5 days; breakdown of the endometrial lining of the uterus and its blood vessels","3–5 days; rupture of the Graafian follicle","10–12 days; breakdown of the myometrium of the uterus","14 days; degeneration of the zona pellucida of the ovum"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Menstrual flow lasts 3–5 days. It forms when the endometrium and its blood vessels break down into a liquid that leaves through the vagina. The myometrium is muscular and does not shed, so option 3 is wrong.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-002","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":2,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Which part of the fallopian tube is funnel-shaped, lies closest to the ovary, and bears finger-like projections at its edges that help collect the ovum after ovulation?","image":"","options":["Ampulla","Isthmus","Infundibulum","Cervical canal"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The infundibulum is the funnel-shaped part of the oviduct nearest the ovary, and its edges carry fimbriae that collect the released ovum. The ampulla is the wider middle part, and the isthmus is the narrow part that joins the uterus.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-003","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":3,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Spermatogenesis in human males begins at the age of puberty.\nReason (R): At puberty there is a significant increase in the secretion of gonadotropin releasing hormone (GnRH).","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The rise in GnRH at puberty increases LH and FSH secretion. LH raises androgen output, which drives spermatogenesis, so R directly explains why spermatogenesis starts at puberty.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-004","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":4,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following cells of the human male germ line are haploid?\nSpermatogonium, primary spermatocyte, secondary spermatocyte, spermatid, spermatozoon","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Spermatogonia and primary spermatocytes are diploid (46 chromosomes). Meiosis I produces haploid secondary spermatocytes (23 chromosomes), so secondary spermatocytes, spermatids and spermatozoa are haploid. The common trap is counting the primary spermatocyte as haploid.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-005","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":5,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"A female gamete is collected from the ampullary region of the fallopian tube just after ovulation, before any sperm has contacted it. Which statement about this cell is correct?","image":"","options":["It is a primary oocyte arrested in prophase I and will complete meiosis I only after sperm entry","It is a haploid ootid that has already given off the second polar body","It is a secondary oocyte that has completed meiosis I; meiosis II will be completed only after sperm entry","It is a diploid secondary oocyte, because the first meiotic division is equational"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Meiosis I is completed in the tertiary follicle, producing a haploid secondary oocyte that is released at ovulation. Sperm entry induces completion of the unequal second meiotic division, yielding the ootid and the second polar body. The ootid and second polar body therefore cannot exist before fertilisation.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-006","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":6,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Approximately how many sperms does a human male ejaculate during a coitus?","image":"","options":["2 to 3 million","20 to 30 million","200 to 300 million","2000 to 3000 million"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"According to NCERT, a human male ejaculates about 200 to 300 million sperms during a coitus. The other options are wrong by a factor of ten or more.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-007","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":7,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Once parturition begins, uterine contractions become progressively stronger until the baby is expelled.\nReason (R): Oxytocin released from the maternal pituitary causes stronger uterine contractions, which in turn stimulate further oxytocin secretion.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The foetal ejection reflex triggers oxytocin release. Oxytocin strengthens uterine contractions, and these contractions stimulate still more oxytocin, forming a positive feedback loop. This loop is exactly why the contractions keep intensifying, so R correctly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-008","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":8,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"The seminal plasma in human males is described as being rich in which of the following combinations?","image":"","options":["Fructose, calcium and certain enzymes","Glucose, sodium and certain hormones","Fructose, iron and androgens","Sucrose, calcium and antibodies"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Seminal plasma is formed by the secretions of the seminal vesicles, the prostate and the bulbourethral glands. It is rich in fructose, calcium and certain enzymes. The common trap is to pick glucose as the sugar instead of fructose.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-009","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":9,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"The milk secreted during the first few days of lactation is especially important for the new-born because it","image":"","options":["contains several antibodies that help the baby develop resistance","is rich in fructose, which supplies energy for motility","contains oxytocin, which stimulates the baby's growth","lacks proteins, so the baby's immature gut can digest it easily"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The milk of the initial days is called colostrum. It contains several antibodies that are essential for building resistance in new-born babies. Fructose is a component of seminal plasma, not milk.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-010","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":10,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Spermatids are changed into spermatozoa by which process?","image":"","options":["Spermiation","Spermatocytogenesis","Spermiogenesis","Second meiotic division"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Spermiogenesis is the transformation of spermatids into sperms. Spermiation is a different step: it is the later release of the formed sperms from the seminiferous tubules.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-011","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":11,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"In the human testis, luteinising hormone (LH) acts directly on which cells?","image":"","options":["Sertoli cells","Spermatogonia","Primary spermatocytes","Leydig cells"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"LH acts on the Leydig (interstitial) cells and stimulates them to make and secrete androgens. FSH, not LH, acts on the Sertoli cells.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-012","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":12,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"During coitus, semen is released by the penis into the vagina. This event is called:","image":"","options":["Fertilisation","Implantation","Insemination","Spermiation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The transfer of semen into the vagina is called insemination. Spermiation is a different process: it is the release of sperms from the seminiferous tubules inside the testis.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-013","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":13,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Oogonia are formed in the fetal ovary during embryonic development, and no new oogonia are added after birth.\nStatement II: The primary oocyte completes its first meiotic division while it is inside the secondary follicle.\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: oogonia form only in fetal life. Statement II is incorrect because the primary oocyte completes meiosis I within the tertiary follicle, the stage that has the antrum, not the secondary follicle.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-014","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":14,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"In human beings, by the end of which month of pregnancy is the embryo's heart formed?","image":"","options":["First month","Second month","Third month","Fifth month"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The embryo's heart forms after one month of pregnancy. The heart sound, heard through a stethoscope, can be the first sign of a growing foetus. Limbs and digits appear by the second month, which is a common mix-up.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-015","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":15,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Which part of a human sperm has numerous mitochondria that supply energy for sperm motility?","image":"","options":["Head","Neck","Middle piece","Acrosome"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The middle piece holds many mitochondria. They produce the energy that drives the tail, so the sperm can move. The tail performs the movement, but the energy is generated in the middle piece.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-016","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":16,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements about early human embryonic development is incorrect?","image":"","options":["The three germ layers give rise to all tissues (organs) of the adult","The ectoderm and endoderm are formed before the mesoderm appears","The trophoblast differentiates into ectoderm, mesoderm and endoderm","Stem cells of the inner cell mass can give rise to all tissues and organs"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The germ layers arise from the inner cell mass, which differentiates as the embryo. The trophoblast does not form them. Instead, it attaches to the endometrium and forms chorionic villi that contribute to the placenta.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-017","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":17,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"In human females, cyclic menstruation is an indicator of the normal reproductive phase. This phase extends between:","image":"","options":["Ovulation and implantation","Menarche and menopause","Puberty and first pregnancy","Menopause and menarche of the next generation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The reproductive phase begins with menarche, the first menstruation at puberty. It ends with menopause, when menstrual cycles stop at around 50 years of age.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-018","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":18,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In a 28-day human menstrual cycle, the Graafian follicle ruptures at about the middle of the cycle.\nReason (R): The level of LH rises rapidly to a maximum at mid-cycle, and this LH surge induces rupture of the Graafian follicle.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Ovulation occurs around day 14. It happens because the mid-cycle LH surge causes the Graafian follicle to rupture, so R correctly explains A. FSH also peaks at this time, but the rupture is attributed to the LH surge.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-019","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":19,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Assume each testis has exactly 250 testicular lobules and each lobule contains the maximum number of seminiferous tubules stated in NCERT. What is the maximum total number of seminiferous tubules in both testes of a man?","image":"","options":["750","500","1500","250"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Each lobule holds 1–3 tubules, so one testis can have at most 250 × 3 = 750 tubules. Two testes give 1500. Choosing 750 is the trap of forgetting that the testes are paired.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-020","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":20,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"A slide shows a cross-section of a testis. Several seminiferous tubules are visible. In the spaces between the tubules, next to small blood vessels, lies a group of cells. Which hormone acts on these cells, and what do they secrete?","image":"","options":["FSH; androgens","LH; androgens","FSH; factors that help spermiogenesis","LH; factors that help spermiogenesis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The cells in the interstitial spaces are Leydig cells. LH stimulates them to make and secrete androgens. FSH acts on the Sertoli cells inside the tubules, which secrete factors that help spermiogenesis.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-021","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":21,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Trophoblast\n(B) Inner cell mass\n(C) Chorionic villi\n(D) Umbilical cord\nList-II\n(i) Differentiates as the embryo\n(ii) Connects the placenta to the embryo\n(iii) Outer layer of blastocyst that attaches to the endometrium\n(iv) Finger-like projections that interdigitate with uterine tissue\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The trophoblast is the outer layer of the blastocyst and attaches to the endometrium. The inner cell mass becomes the embryo. Chorionic villi arise from the trophoblast and interdigitate with uterine tissue to form the placenta. The umbilical cord links the placenta and the embryo. A common trap is swapping the trophoblast with the inner cell mass.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-022","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":22,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"The birth canal in the human female is formed by:","image":"","options":["Fallopian tube and uterine cavity","Uterine cavity and cervical canal","Cervical canal and vagina","Vagina and labia minora"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The cavity of the cervix (the cervical canal) together with the vagina forms the birth canal. The uterine cavity holds the foetus but is not counted as part of the birth canal.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-023","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":23,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: During the follicular phase, the secretion of LH and FSH increases gradually and stimulates follicular development as well as secretion of estrogens by the growing follicles.\nStatement II: Regeneration of the endometrium through proliferation takes place during the luteal phase, after the corpus luteum has formed.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I describes the follicular phase correctly. The endometrium regenerates by proliferation during the follicular phase, alongside follicle growth, not during the luteal phase. In the luteal phase, progesterone only maintains the endometrium.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-024","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":24,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. GnRH\nB. LH\nC. FSH\nD. Androgens\nList-II\n(i) Secreted by Leydig cells; stimulate spermatogenesis\n(ii) Acts on Sertoli cells to promote secretion of factors aiding spermiogenesis\n(iii) Hypothalamic hormone acting on the anterior pituitary\n(iv) Acts on Leydig cells\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"GnRH from the hypothalamus stimulates the anterior pituitary. LH acts on Leydig cells, which then secrete androgens that stimulate spermatogenesis. FSH acts on Sertoli cells. Swapping the targets of LH and FSH is the usual error.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-025","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":25,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (phase of menstrual cycle) with List-II (event):\nList-I\nA. Menstrual phase\nB. Follicular phase\nC. Ovulatory phase\nD. Luteal phase\nList-II\n(i) Endometrium regenerates by proliferation as follicles mature\n(ii) Corpus luteum secretes large amounts of progesterone\n(iii) Endometrial lining and its blood vessels break down\n(iv) LH surge causes rupture of the Graafian follicle\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The endometrium breaks down in the menstrual phase. Follicles mature and the endometrium proliferates in the follicular phase. The LH surge ruptures the Graafian follicle at ovulation. The corpus luteum secretes progesterone in the luteal phase.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-026","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":26,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following pairs are correctly matched?\n(i) Morula – embryo with 8 to 16 blastomeres\n(ii) Trophoblast – differentiates into the embryo\n(iii) Chorionic villi – finger-like projections of the trophoblast\n(iv) Implantation – embedding of the blastocyst in the wall of the fallopian tube\n(v) Cleavage – mitotic divisions of the zygote","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Pairs (i), (iii) and (v) are correct. The inner cell mass, not the trophoblast, forms the embryo, and implantation occurs in the uterine endometrium, not the oviduct.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-027","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":27,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly states when and how the sex of a human baby is decided?","image":"","options":["At fertilisation, depending on whether the fertilising sperm carries an X or a Y chromosome","At fertilisation, depending on whether the ovum carries an X or a Y chromosome","At implantation, depending on the hormones produced by the trophoblast","At the end of the first trimester, when the external genital organs develop"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Every ovum carries an X chromosome, while half of all sperms carry X and half carry Y. The sex of the zygote (XX or XY) is therefore fixed at fertilisation by the sperm. Ova never carry a Y chromosome.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-028","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":28,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. Sertoli cells\nB. Leydig cells\nC. Acrosome\nD. Middle piece of sperm\nList-II\nI. Enzymes that help the sperm enter the ovum\nII. Synthesis and secretion of androgens\nIII. Mitochondria that supply energy for tail movement\nIV. Nutrition to the male germ cells\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Sertoli cells nourish germ cells, Leydig cells in the interstitial spaces secrete androgens, the acrosome holds enzymes for fertilisation, and the middle piece carries mitochondria. A common trap is swapping the roles of Sertoli and Leydig cells.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-029","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":29,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements regarding parturition and lactation is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Signals for parturition originate from the fully developed foetus and the placenta","Mild uterine contractions of the foetal ejection reflex trigger release of oxytocin from the maternal pituitary","Relaxin, secreted in the later phase of pregnancy, is produced by the placenta","Colostrum, the milk of the first few days, contains antibodies that help the new-born develop resistance"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Relaxin is secreted by the ovary in late pregnancy, not by the placenta. It is easy to confuse with hCG and hPL, which the placenta does make. The other statements correctly describe the oxytocin-driven positive feedback of parturition and the nature of colostrum.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-030","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":30,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (phase of menstrual cycle) with List-II (characteristic event):\nList-I\n(A) Menstrual phase\n(B) Follicular phase\n(C) Ovulatory phase\n(D) Luteal phase\nList-II\n(I) Remaining parts of Graafian follicle form corpus luteum\n(II) Primary follicles grow into a mature Graafian follicle\n(III) Disintegration of endometrium and its blood vessels\n(IV) LH surge releases the ovum\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The pairings are:\n- Menstrual phase: endometrium breaks down.\n- Follicular phase: primary follicles mature into a Graafian follicle.\n- Ovulatory phase: the LH surge releases the ovum.\n- Luteal phase: the corpus luteum forms.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-031","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":31,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"In the human female, the oogonia that have entered meiosis are temporarily arrested as primary oocytes. At which stage are they arrested?","image":"","options":["Metaphase of mitosis","Prophase-I of meiosis","Anaphase-I of meiosis","Prophase-II of meiosis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Oogonia formed in the fetal ovary begin meiosis and are temporarily arrested in prophase-I; these arrested cells are called primary oocytes. The arrest is not in a mitotic stage or in meiosis II.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-032","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":32,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Immediately after implantation, the inner cell mass differentiates into an outer and an inner layer. The germ layer that soon appears between these two layers is the","image":"","options":["trophoblast","endoderm","mesoderm","chorion"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The inner cell mass first forms an outer ectoderm and an inner endoderm. The mesoderm then appears between them. The trophoblast is the outer layer of the blastocyst, not a germ layer.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-033","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":33,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following hormones is secreted by the ovary, rather than the placenta, in the later phase of human pregnancy?","image":"","options":["Human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG)","Human placental lactogen (hPL)","Relaxin","Oxytocin"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In the later phase of pregnancy, relaxin is secreted by the ovary. hCG and hPL are placental hormones. Oxytocin comes from the maternal pituitary.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-034","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":34,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"In an adult human male, each testis is oval in shape with approximate dimensions of","image":"","options":["length 4–5 cm, width 2–3 cm","length 2–3 cm, width 1–2 cm","length 10–12 cm, width 4–5 cm","length 2–4 cm, width 4–5 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"An adult testis is about 4–5 cm long and 2–3 cm wide. The 2–4 cm figure in one option is the length of an ovary, and 10–12 cm is the length of a fallopian tube.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-035","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":35,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"In the human female, the part of the fallopian tube lying closest to the ovary is funnel-shaped. What is this part called?","image":"","options":["Ampulla","Isthmus","Infundibulum","Cervix"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The infundibulum is the funnel-shaped part of the oviduct next to the ovary. The ampulla is the wider middle part, and the isthmus is the narrow part that joins the uterus.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-036","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":36,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(a) Each mammary lobe contains clusters of cells called alveoli whose cells secrete milk.\n(b) The mammary tubules of each lobe join directly to form the lactiferous duct.\n(c) Several mammary ducts join to form a wider mammary ampulla.\n(d) The glandular tissue of each breast is divided into about 250 lobules.\n(e) The vaginal opening is often partially covered by a membrane called the hymen.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements (a), (c) and (e) are correct. In (b), the tubules first form a mammary duct, not the lactiferous duct. In (d), the breast has 15–20 lobes; 250 is the number of lobules in a testis.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-037","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":37,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (hormone) with List-II (site/role in male reproduction):\nList-I\nA. GnRH\nB. LH\nC. FSH\nD. Androgens\nList-II\nI. Acts on Leydig cells\nII. Acts on the anterior pituitary\nIII. Directly stimulate the process of spermatogenesis\nIV. Acts on Sertoli cells to release factors that aid spermiogenesis\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Hypothalamic GnRH acts on the anterior pituitary. There, LH targets Leydig cells to produce androgens, which stimulate spermatogenesis, while FSH targets Sertoli cells. The common trap is swapping the targets of LH and FSH.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-038","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":38,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Semen analysis reports of four men are given below (total sperm count; % sperms with normal shape and size; % sperms with vigorous motility). Whose report meets the NCERT criteria for normal fertility?","image":"","options":["Man P: 250 million; 55%; 50%","Man Q: 280 million; 65%; 45%","Man R: 210 million; 70%; 30%","Man S: 300 million; 58%; 42%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Normal fertility needs at least 60% sperms of normal shape and size and at least 40% with vigorous motility. Only Q satisfies both conditions. P and S fall short on morphology, and R falls short on motility, even though all four counts are in the normal range.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-039","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":39,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"A diagram of an early human embryo inside the uterus shows an outer layer of cells. A small group of cells is attached to the inner side of this outer layer at one end. What will this attached group of cells form?","image":"","options":["Chorionic villi","Placenta","The embryo","Umbilical cord"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The attached group of cells is the inner cell mass of the blastocyst, and it differentiates into the embryo. The outer layer is the trophoblast, which attaches to the endometrium and later forms chorionic villi.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-040","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":40,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (layer or region) with List-II (feature):\nList-I\nA. Perimetrium\nB. Myometrium\nC. Endometrium\nD. Ovarian medulla\nList-II\nI. Inner glandular layer showing cyclical changes during menstrual cycle\nII. External thin membranous layer of uterus\nIII. Inner zone of ovarian stroma\nIV. Thick smooth muscle layer showing strong contraction during delivery\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The perimetrium is the outer thin membrane and the myometrium is the thick smooth muscle that contracts strongly at birth. The endometrium is the glandular lining that changes cyclically, and the medulla is the inner zone of the ovarian stroma. A common trap is swapping the roles of the myometrium and endometrium.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-041","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":41,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (part of uterus) with List-II (feature):\nList-I\nA. Perimetrium\nB. Myometrium\nC. Endometrium\nD. Cervix\nList-II\nI. Glandular inner lining showing cyclical changes\nII. Narrow passage through which the uterus opens into the vagina\nIII. External thin membranous layer\nIV. Thick smooth muscle layer that contracts strongly during delivery\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The perimetrium is the outer membrane and the myometrium is the muscular middle layer active in parturition. The endometrium is the glandular lining that changes during the cycle, and the uterus opens into the vagina through the cervix.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-042","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":42,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Leydig cells, along with Sertoli cells, line the inside of the seminiferous tubules.\nStatement II: The interstitial spaces of the testis contain small blood vessels and immunologically competent cells.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The seminiferous tubules are lined by spermatogonia and Sertoli cells. Leydig cells lie outside the tubules in the interstitial spaces, which also contain blood vessels and immunologically competent cells.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-043","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":43,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"In the human fetal ovary, oogonia enter meiosis and then halt. At which stage does this temporary arrest occur, giving rise to primary oocytes?","image":"","options":["Metaphase II","Prophase I","Anaphase I","Telophase II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Oogonia start meiosis during fetal life and are temporarily arrested in prophase I. These arrested cells are called primary oocytes. Metaphase II arrest describes the secondary oocyte, not the primary oocyte.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-044","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":44,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"A secondary spermatocyte in the human testis contains how many chromosomes?","image":"","options":["46","23","92","44"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The first meiotic division is a reduction division, so each secondary spermatocyte formed from a primary spermatocyte is haploid with 23 chromosomes. 46 is the diploid number of spermatogonia and primary spermatocytes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-045","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":45,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"The first menstruation that begins at puberty in a human female is called","image":"","options":["Menopause","Menarche","Ovulation","Parturition"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Menarche is the first menstruation, occurring at puberty. Menopause is the stopping of menstrual cycles around 50 years of age, and ovulation is the release of the ovum.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-046","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":46,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The menstrual cycle is the reproductive cycle found in all female mammals.\nStatement II: In human beings, menstrual cycles cease around 50 years of age, and this is termed menopause.\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The menstrual cycle occurs only in female primates such as monkeys, apes and humans, not in all mammals. Statement II correctly describes menopause at around 50 years.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-047","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":47,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"In an adult man, the secretion of GnRH by the hypothalamus is completely blocked by a drug. Which of the following is the most likely consequence?","image":"","options":["Only LH secretion falls; FSH secretion and Sertoli cell function remain normal","Androgen secretion rises because Sertoli cells take over androgen synthesis","Secretion of both LH and FSH falls, so androgen production by Leydig cells and spermatogenesis both decline","Spermatogenesis continues normally because the meiosis of spermatocytes does not depend on hormones"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"GnRH stimulates the anterior pituitary to release both LH and FSH. Without it, Leydig cells receive no LH and make fewer androgens, and Sertoli cells receive no FSH. Spermatogenesis therefore declines, and Sertoli cells do not make androgens in any case.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-048","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":48,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The cervical canal forms part of the birth canal.\nReason (R): The cavity of the cervix, together with the vagina, forms the birth canal through which the baby is expelled during parturition.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The uterus opens into the vagina through the narrow cervix. The cervical canal together with the vagina makes up the birth canal, so R directly explains why A is true.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-049","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":49,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Each human ovary is about 2 to 4 cm long and is connected to the pelvic wall and the uterus by ligaments.\nStatement II: The human uterus is a paired, inverted pear-shaped organ supported by ligaments attached to the pelvic wall.\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I correctly gives the size of the ovary and its ligament attachments. Statement II is wrong because the uterus is a single organ, even though it is inverted pear-shaped and supported by ligaments.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-050","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":50,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\nA. Mons pubis\nB. Labia majora\nC. Labia minora\nD. Clitoris\nList-II\nI. Paired folds of tissue lying under the larger outer folds\nII. Tiny finger-like structure above the urethral opening\nIII. Cushion of fatty tissue covered by skin and pubic hair\nIV. Fleshy folds extending down from the mons pubis and surrounding the vaginal opening\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The mons pubis is a fatty cushion, and the labia majora are fleshy folds surrounding the vaginal opening. The labia minora lie under the labia majora, and the clitoris lies above the urethral opening. Swapping the two labia is the key trap.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-051","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":51,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"The mons pubis is best described as:","image":"","options":["Paired folds of tissue lying beneath the labia majora","A membrane that partially covers the vaginal opening","A tiny finger-like erectile structure","A cushion of fatty tissue covered by skin and pubic hair"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The mons pubis is a fatty cushion covered by skin and pubic hair. The other options describe the labia minora, the hymen and the clitoris.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-052","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":52,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (hormone) with List-II (its target / action as described in NCERT):\nList-I\nA. LH\nB. FSH\nC. Oxytocin\nD. Progesterone\nList-II\nI. Acts on Sertoli cells to promote spermiogenesis\nII. Maintains the endometrium\nIII. Acts on Leydig cells to stimulate androgen secretion\nIV. Causes stronger contractions of uterine muscle\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"LH acts on Leydig cells and FSH acts on Sertoli cells. Oxytocin strengthens myometrial contractions during parturition, and progesterone from the corpus luteum maintains the endometrium. Students often swap LH and FSH targets.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-053","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":53,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following hormones are produced in women only during pregnancy?\nhCG, estrogens, hPL, prolactin, relaxin, cortisol, progestogens, thyroxine","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Only hCG, hPL and relaxin are produced exclusively during pregnancy, which makes three. Estrogens, progestogens, prolactin, cortisol and thyroxine are present in non-pregnant women too; their levels simply rise several-fold during pregnancy.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-054","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":54,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following hormones is NOT produced in women only during pregnancy, but is instead present at increased levels in the maternal blood during pregnancy?","image":"","options":["Human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG)","Human placental lactogen (hPL)","Relaxin","Prolactin"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT states that hCG, hPL and relaxin are produced in women only during pregnancy. Prolactin is also made outside pregnancy; its level in maternal blood simply rises several-fold during pregnancy.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-055","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":55,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Which type of ovarian follicle is first characterised by a fluid-filled cavity called the antrum?","image":"","options":["Tertiary follicle","Primary follicle","Secondary follicle","Corpus luteum"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The antrum, a fluid-filled cavity, is the defining feature of the tertiary follicle. At this stage the theca also becomes organised into theca interna and theca externa. Secondary follicles have extra granulosa layers and a theca but no antrum.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-056","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":56,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"The enlarged end of the penis, called the glans penis, is covered by:","image":"","options":["A loose fold of skin called foreskin","The scrotum","A dense covering called tunica","The urethral meatus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The glans penis is covered by a loose fold of skin called the foreskin. The urethral meatus is only the external opening of the urethra, and the scrotum is the pouch that holds the testes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-057","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":57,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"After insemination, sperms travel from the vagina to the site of fertilisation. Which sequence correctly shows the structures they pass through?","image":"","options":["Vagina → cervical canal → uterine cavity → isthmus → ampulla","Vagina → uterine cavity → cervical canal → ampulla → isthmus","Vagina → cervical canal → uterine cavity → ampulla → infundibulum","Vagina → cervical canal → isthmus → uterine cavity → ampulla"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Sperms pass through the cervix into the uterus, then enter the oviduct at its uterine end (the isthmus), and reach the ampulla, where fertilisation occurs. The infundibulum lies beyond the ampulla, near the ovary, so sperms do not need to reach it.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-058","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":58,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"In a woman with a regular 28-day cycle, a structure in the ovary on day 20 is secreting large amounts of progesterone, and the uterine lining is thick and maintained. Fertilisation does not occur in this cycle. Which sequence of events will most likely follow?","image":"","options":["LH surge → rupture of the structure → fall in estrogen → menstruation","Corpus luteum persists → progesterone rises further → menstruation stops","Graafian follicle forms from the structure → endometrium proliferates → ovulation","Corpus luteum degenerates → progesterone falls → endometrium disintegrates → menstruation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The progesterone-secreting structure in the luteal phase is the corpus luteum. Without fertilisation it degenerates, so progesterone support is withdrawn and the endometrium breaks down, causing menstruation and starting a new cycle. The corpus luteum persists only if pregnancy occurs.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-059","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":59,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Which part of the sperm releases secretions that help it pass through the zona pellucida and the plasma membrane into the cytoplasm of the ovum?","image":"","options":["Middle piece","Tail","Neck","Acrosome"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The acrosome is a cap over the sperm head and is filled with enzymes. Its secretions help the sperm enter the ovum. The middle piece only supplies energy, through its mitochondria, for the movement of the tail.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-060","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":60,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"A section through the human uterine wall is examined from the outside towards the uterine cavity. Which option correctly gives the order of layers AND the layer whose breakdown produces menstrual flow?","image":"","options":["Endometrium → myometrium → perimetrium; myometrium breaks down","Perimetrium → myometrium → endometrium; endometrium breaks down","Perimetrium → endometrium → myometrium; endometrium breaks down","Myometrium → perimetrium → endometrium; perimetrium breaks down"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"From outside to inside, the layers are the thin perimetrium, the muscular myometrium and the glandular endometrium lining the cavity. Menstrual flow results from breakdown of the endometrium and its blood vessels; the myometrium is not shed.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-061","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":61,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about human oogenesis is correct?","image":"","options":["New oogonia continue to be added to the ovary from puberty until menopause","The primary oocyte completes its first meiotic division within the tertiary follicle, forming a large secondary oocyte and a tiny first polar body","The zona pellucida is first formed around the primary oocyte of the primary follicle","The second meiotic division of the secondary oocyte is completed before ovulation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Meiosis I is completed in the tertiary follicle and is unequal. No oogonia form after birth, and the zona pellucida forms around the secondary oocyte in the Graafian follicle. Meiosis II is completed only after sperm entry.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-062","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":62,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The rapid rise of LH to its maximum level at mid-cycle (LH surge) induces rupture of the Graafian follicle and release of the ovum.\nStatement II: The corpus luteum secretes large amounts of estrogen, which is essential for maintenance of the endometrium.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The LH surge at about day 14 causes ovulation. The corpus luteum mainly secretes progesterone, not estrogen, to maintain the endometrium. Estrogens come chiefly from the growing follicles.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-063","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":63,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Semen is best described as:","image":"","options":["Secretions of the seminal vesicles alone","Sperms suspended in the secretions of Sertoli cells","Seminal plasma together with the sperms","Secretions of the bulbourethral glands used for lubrication"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Semen consists of the seminal plasma plus the sperms. The seminal plasma is the secretion of the seminal vesicles, prostate and bulbourethral glands. Seminal plasma alone, without sperms, is not semen.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-064","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":64,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Which cells of the human testis synthesise and secrete androgens?","image":"","options":["Sertoli cells","Spermatogonia","Leydig cells","Immunologically competent cells"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Leydig (interstitial) cells lie in the spaces outside the seminiferous tubules and secrete androgens. Sertoli cells are often confused with them, but Sertoli cells nourish the germ cells.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-065","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":65,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A functional mammary gland is characteristic of all female mammals, and the mammary glands are regarded as female secondary sexual characteristics.\nStatement II: Human breasts are composed entirely of glandular tissue, with no fat content.\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct as NCERT gives it. Statement II is incorrect because the breasts contain glandular tissue along with a variable amount of fat.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-066","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":66,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Which feature distinguishes a tertiary follicle from a secondary follicle in the human ovary?","image":"","options":["Presence of a single layer of granulosa cells","Presence of a fluid-filled cavity called antrum","Absence of any theca layer","Presence of a haploid ootid"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A tertiary follicle is characterised by a fluid-filled cavity, the antrum, and its theca is organised into a theca interna and a theca externa. A single granulosa layer describes a primary follicle, and an ootid forms only after fertilisation.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-067","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":67,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Menstruation does not occur during pregnancy.\nReason (R): During pregnancy, the corpus luteum degenerates soon after implantation.\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"All events of the menstrual cycle stop during pregnancy, so A is true. R is false because the corpus luteum degenerates only when fertilisation does not occur. That degeneration is what leads to menstruation, and pregnancy needs a maintained endometrium.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-068","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":68,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"In the human mammary gland, the milk secreted by the alveolar cells is stored in the:","image":"","options":["Cavities (lumens) of the alveoli","Mammary tubules","Lactiferous duct","Nipple"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Alveolar cells secrete milk, and the milk is stored in the lumens of the alveoli themselves. The tubules, ducts and nipple form the pathway that carries milk out.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-069","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":69,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is a recommended practice for menstrual hygiene?","image":"","options":["Using the same sanitary napkin for the whole day to reduce waste","Flushing used sanitary napkins down the toilet drainpipe","Changing sanitary napkins or clean homemade pads every 4–5 hours as required","Avoiding bathing during the menstrual phase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Napkins or clean homemade pads should be changed about every 4–5 hours as needed. Used napkins should be wrapped in used paper before disposal, never thrown into toilet drainpipes. Regular bathing is advised.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-070","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":70,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: For normal fertility, at least 60 per cent of the ejaculated sperms must have normal shape and size.\nStatement II: For normal fertility, at least 60 per cent of the ejaculated sperms must show vigorous motility.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT sets two separate thresholds for normal fertility. At least 60 per cent of sperms must have normal shape and size, and at least 40 per cent (not 60 per cent) must show vigorous motility. Mixing up the two figures is the trap.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-071","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":71,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Which layer of the human uterine wall shows strong contractions during delivery of the baby?","image":"","options":["Perimetrium","Myometrium","Endometrium","Ovarian stroma"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The myometrium is the thick middle layer of smooth muscle, and it contracts strongly during childbirth. The endometrium is the glandular lining that changes cyclically, and the perimetrium is the thin outer membrane.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-072","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":72,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"In the human testis, spermatids are transformed into spermatozoa. What is this process called?","image":"","options":["Spermiation","Spermiogenesis","Spermatogenesis","Second meiotic division"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Spermiogenesis is the transformation of spermatids into spermatozoa. Spermiation is the later release of sperms from the seminiferous tubules. Spermatogenesis is the whole process that begins with spermatogonia.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-073","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":73,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Rete testis\n(B) Epididymis\n(C) Vas deferens\n(D) Urethral meatus\nList-II\n(i) Ascends to the abdomen and loops over the urinary bladder\n(ii) Located along the posterior surface of each testis\n(iii) External opening of the urethra\n(iv) Connects seminiferous tubules to vasa efferentia\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The rete testis links the seminiferous tubules to the vasa efferentia, and the epididymis lies along the back of the testis. The vas deferens loops over the urinary bladder, and the urethral meatus is the outer opening of the urethra.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-074","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":74,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Read the following developmental events of a human foetus:\nP. Limbs and digits develop\nQ. Heart is formed\nR. Eyelids separate and eyelashes are formed\nS. First fetal movements and hair on the head are observed\nWhich option gives the correct chronological order of these events?","image":"","options":["Q → P → S → R","P → Q → S → R","Q → P → R → S","Q → S → P → R"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The heart forms after one month, and limbs and digits develop by the end of the second month. First movements and head hair appear in the fifth month, while the eyelids separate by about 24 weeks (end of the second trimester). The trap is placing eyelid separation before the fifth-month events.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-075","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":75,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly traces the path of sperms from their site of formation to the outside of the body?","image":"","options":["Seminiferous tubules → vasa efferentia → rete testis → epididymis → vas deferens → ejaculatory duct → urethra","Seminiferous tubules → rete testis → vasa efferentia → epididymis → vas deferens → ejaculatory duct → urethra","Seminiferous tubules → rete testis → epididymis → vasa efferentia → vas deferens → urethra → ejaculatory duct","Seminiferous tubules → epididymis → rete testis → vasa efferentia → ejaculatory duct → vas deferens → urethra"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Seminiferous tubules open into the vasa efferentia through the rete testis. The vasa efferentia lead to the epididymis, then the vas deferens, which joins the seminal vesicle duct to form the ejaculatory duct opening into the urethra. The usual trap is swapping rete testis and vasa efferentia.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-076","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":76,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"In human females, the second meiotic division of the secondary oocyte is completed:","image":"","options":["at the time of ovulation, triggered by the LH surge","while the oocyte is still inside the tertiary follicle","when a sperm enters the oocyte during fertilisation","after the blastocyst implants in the endometrium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Sperm entry into the secondary oocyte triggers completion of meiosis II. This is an unequal division that gives a second polar body and a haploid ootid. Ovulation releases the oocyte while it is still a secondary oocyte; only meiosis I is completed in the tertiary follicle.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-077","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":77,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly traces the path of milk from its site of secretion to the outside during breastfeeding?","image":"","options":["Alveoli → Mammary duct → Mammary tubules → Mammary ampulla → Lactiferous duct → Nipple","Alveoli → Mammary tubules → Mammary ampulla → Mammary duct → Lactiferous duct → Nipple","Alveoli → Mammary tubules → Mammary duct → Mammary ampulla → Lactiferous duct → Nipple","Alveoli → Lactiferous duct → Mammary duct → Mammary tubules → Mammary ampulla → Nipple"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Alveoli open into mammary tubules, and the tubules of each lobe join to form a mammary duct. Several ducts then join to form the wider mammary ampulla, which connects to the lactiferous duct opening at the nipple. The usual trap is reversing the duct and ampulla.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-078","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":78,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about human fertilisation are correct?\n(i) Contact of a sperm with the zona pellucida induces membrane changes that block the entry of additional sperms.\n(ii) Fertilisation normally occurs in the isthmus of the oviduct.\n(iii) The ootid formed after the second meiotic division is haploid.\n(iv) Fusion of the haploid nuclei of the sperm and the ovum produces a diploid zygote.\n(v) The sex of the baby is decided only at the time of implantation.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (iii) and (iv) are correct. Statement (ii) is wrong because fertilisation occurs in the ampulla; the isthmus is where cleavage begins as the zygote moves towards the uterus. Statement (v) is wrong because sex is already fixed at fertilisation.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-079","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":79,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"The seminal plasma of human males is rich in:","image":"","options":["Glucose, sodium and certain hormones","Sucrose, potassium and antibodies","Fructose, calcium and certain enzymes","Lactose, iron and mucus only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The combined secretions of the seminal vesicles, prostate and bulbourethral glands form the seminal plasma. This fluid is rich in fructose, calcium and certain enzymes. Glucose is a common wrong choice because it is the familiar body sugar.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-080","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":80,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The menstrual cycle is the reproductive cycle of female primates such as monkeys, apes and humans.\n(ii) Two ova are normally released during the middle of each human menstrual cycle.\n(iii) Absence of menstruation always confirms pregnancy.\n(iv) Sanitary napkins or clean homemade pads should be changed every 4–5 hours as per requirement.\n(v) Used sanitary napkins should be wrapped in used paper for disposal and not thrown into toilet drainpipes.\n(vi) Both LH and FSH attain peak levels around the 14th day of the cycle.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (iv), (v) and (vi) are correct, giving four in all. Only one ovum is released per cycle, so (ii) is wrong. Missed menstruation may also result from stress or poor health, so (iii) is wrong.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-081","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":81,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Suppose a woman takes a drug that specifically prevents the mid-cycle LH surge, while gonadotropin secretion otherwise rises gradually in the follicular phase as normal. Which outcome is most likely in that cycle?","image":"","options":["Ovulation occurs normally, but the corpus luteum fails to secrete progesterone","The Graafian follicle does not rupture, so no ovum is released and no corpus luteum forms to secrete large amounts of progesterone","Follicles fail to develop beyond the primary stage and estrogens are never secreted","The menstrual phase is skipped and the endometrium is maintained as in pregnancy"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The LH surge is what ruptures the Graafian follicle. Without it, ovulation fails, and the corpus luteum, which forms from the remains of the ruptured follicle, cannot develop. Follicle growth and estrogen secretion depend on the gradual gonadotropin rise, which is unaffected.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-082","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":82,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Where is the clitoris located in the human female?","image":"","options":["Between the vaginal opening and the anus","At the lower junction of the two labia majora, below the vaginal opening","At the upper junction of the two labia minora, above the urethral opening","Inside the vaginal opening, partly covered by the hymen"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The clitoris is a tiny finger-like structure. It lies at the upper junction of the two labia minora, above the urethral opening.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-083","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":83,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about pregnancy, parturition and lactation is incorrect?","image":"","options":["The signals for parturition originate from the fully developed foetus and the placenta, inducing mild contractions called foetal ejection reflex","Oxytocin released from the maternal pituitary causes stronger uterine contractions, which in turn stimulate further oxytocin secretion","Relaxin is secreted by the placenta in the later phase of pregnancy","Colostrum, produced in the initial days of lactation, contains antibodies essential for developing resistance in the new-born"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"According to NCERT, relaxin is secreted by the ovary in the later phase of pregnancy, not by the placenta. The placenta produces hCG, hPL, estrogens and progestogens; the other statements correctly describe the positive feedback in parturition and the role of colostrum.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-084","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":84,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The scrotum keeps the testes about 2–2.5 °C below the normal internal body temperature, which is necessary for spermatogenesis.\nStatement II: Like ovum formation in women, sperm formation in men stops at around fifty years of age.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The scrotum does maintain the lower testicular temperature needed for spermatogenesis. However, sperm formation continues even in old men; only ovum formation ceases, at around fifty years of age.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-085","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":85,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are female accessory ducts?\nOvary, fallopian tube, uterus, vagina, mammary gland, labia minora","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Only the oviducts (fallopian tubes), the uterus and the vagina are the female accessory ducts. The ovary is the primary sex organ, the mammary gland is an accessory gland, and the labia minora are part of the external genitalia.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-086","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":86,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"A secondary oocyte in a woman is entered by a sperm carrying a Y chromosome. Which of the following correctly describes the cells produced as a direct result of this event?","image":"","options":["The second polar body has 23 chromosomes including a Y; the zygote has 46 chromosomes with XX","The ootid has 46 chromosomes; the zygote has 92 chromosomes with XXYY","The second polar body has 46 chromosomes; the zygote has 23 chromosomes with Y","The second polar body has 23 chromosomes including an X; the zygote has 46 chromosomes with XY"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Meiosis II of the oocyte happens entirely within the female's cells, so both the ootid and the second polar body are haploid (23 chromosomes) and each carries an X. The haploid ootid nucleus then fuses with the Y-bearing sperm nucleus to give a 46, XY zygote, which develops as a male. The polar body can never receive the sperm's Y chromosome.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-087","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":87,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (structure) with List-II (feature/role) in the male reproductive system.\nList-I\n(A) Vasa efferentia\n(B) Epididymis\n(C) Ejaculatory duct\n(D) Bulbourethral glands\nList-II\n(I) Formed after the vas deferens receives a duct from the seminal vesicle; opens into the urethra\n(II) Secretion helps in lubrication of the penis\n(III) Carry sperms out of the testis from the rete testis\n(IV) Located along the posterior surface of each testis\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Sperms pass from the seminiferous tubules through the rete testis into the vasa efferentia, which leave the testis. The vasa efferentia open into the epididymis, which lies along the posterior surface of the testis. The vas deferens receives the seminal vesicle duct and opens into the urethra as the ejaculatory duct, while bulbourethral secretions lubricate the penis.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-088","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":88,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (developmental event in the human foetus) with List-II (time by which it is usually seen).\nList-I\nA. Formation of the heart\nB. Development of limbs and digits\nC. First movements of the foetus and hair on the head\nD. Separation of eyelids and formation of eyelashes\nList-II\nI. End of about 24 weeks\nII. Fifth month\nIII. After one month\nIV. End of second month","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The heart forms after one month, and limbs and digits by the end of the second month. Foetal movements and head hair appear in the fifth month. Eyelids separate and eyelashes form by the end of about 24 weeks, which is the end of the second trimester.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-089","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":89,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about hormones during human pregnancy are correct?\n(a) hCG is produced in women only during pregnancy.\n(b) hPL is produced by the placenta.\n(c) Cortisol levels in maternal blood rise several-fold.\n(d) Thyroxine levels in maternal blood fall during pregnancy.\n(e) Relaxin is secreted by the placenta in the later phase of pregnancy.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements (a), (b) and (c) are correct, so three statements are correct. Statement (d) is wrong because thyroxine, like cortisol and prolactin, increases several-fold during pregnancy. Statement (e) is wrong because relaxin is secreted by the ovary, not the placenta.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-090","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":90,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Each human testis is divided into compartments. Which option correctly gives the approximate number of these compartments and the number of seminiferous tubules in each?","image":"","options":["About 250 testicular lobules, each with one to three seminiferous tubules","About 250 testicular lobules, each with ten to fifteen seminiferous tubules","About 15–20 testicular lobules, each with one to three seminiferous tubules","About 2500 testicular lobules, each with a single seminiferous tubule"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Each testis has about 250 testicular lobules, and each lobule contains one to three highly coiled seminiferous tubules. The figure 15–20 refers to mammary lobes, not testicular lobules.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-091","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":91,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following shows the correct order of cells formed during spermatogenesis?","image":"","options":["Spermatogonium → primary spermatocyte → secondary spermatocyte → spermatid → spermatozoon","Spermatogonium → secondary spermatocyte → primary spermatocyte → spermatid → spermatozoon","Primary spermatocyte → spermatogonium → secondary spermatocyte → spermatozoon → spermatid","Spermatogonium → primary spermatocyte → spermatid → secondary spermatocyte → spermatozoon"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Spermatogonia multiply by mitosis, and some of them become primary spermatocytes. Meiosis I then gives secondary spermatocytes, meiosis II gives spermatids, and spermiogenesis turns spermatids into spermatozoa.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-092","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":92,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"During which month of human pregnancy are the first movements of the foetus and the appearance of hair on the head usually observed?","image":"","options":["Second month","Fifth month","Third month","Seventh month"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"First foetal movements and head hair usually appear during the fifth month. By the end of the second month, limbs and digits develop, and by the end of the first trimester most major organ systems are formed.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-093","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":93,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): During parturition, uterine contractions become progressively stronger until the baby is expelled.\nReason (R): Oxytocin causes stronger uterine contractions, and these contractions in turn stimulate further secretion of oxytocin.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The reflex between uterine contraction and oxytocin secretion is a positive feedback loop. Each round produces stronger contractions, which explains the build-up leading to expulsion of the baby.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-094","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":94,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The cell released from the human ovary at ovulation is a secondary oocyte.\nReason (R): The oocyte completes its second meiotic division while still inside the Graafian follicle.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Ovulation releases the secondary oocyte, so A is true. Meiosis II is completed only when a sperm enters the oocyte, producing the second polar body and the ootid, so R is false.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-095","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":95,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In human females, oogonia are formed in the fetal ovary and no more oogonia are added after birth.\nStatement II: The number of primary follicles present in each ovary at puberty is greater than the number of oogonia formed in that ovary during fetal life.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"About a couple of million oogonia form in each fetal ovary. Many follicles then degenerate between birth and puberty, leaving only 60,000–80,000 primary follicles per ovary, so the number falls rather than rises. Statement I is correct and Statement II is incorrect.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-096","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":96,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The secondary oocyte is much larger than the first polar body formed alongside it.\nReason (R): The first meiotic division of the primary oocyte is unequal, and the secondary oocyte retains most of the nutrient-rich cytoplasm.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Meiosis I in the oocyte divides the cytoplasm unequally. The secondary oocyte keeps the bulk of the cytoplasm, while the first polar body receives very little, so R directly explains why the secondary oocyte is larger.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-097","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":97,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Which event directly induces the rupture of the Graafian follicle and the release of the ovum in the middle of the menstrual cycle?","image":"","options":["A sharp fall in progesterone secretion","Rapid secretion of LH reaching its maximum level (LH surge)","Degeneration of the corpus luteum","A gradual decline in FSH secretion"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"LH rises to its peak around day 14. This mid-cycle LH surge causes the Graafian follicle to rupture and release the ovum. A fall in progesterone and degeneration of the corpus luteum belong to the end of the cycle, not ovulation.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-098","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":98,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the human testis are correct?\n(i) The male reproductive system is located in the pelvis region.\n(ii) An adult testis is oval, about 4–5 cm long and 2–3 cm wide.\n(iii) Sertoli cells are located in the interstitial spaces between seminiferous tubules.\n(iv) Immunologically competent cells are present in the interstitial spaces along with Leydig cells.\n(v) The inner lining of a seminiferous tubule is formed by spermatogonia and Leydig cells.\n(vi) Each testis is covered by a dense covering.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (ii), (iv) and (vi) are correct, which makes four. Sertoli cells line the inside of the seminiferous tubules together with spermatogonia, while Leydig cells lie outside the tubules in the interstitial spaces. This makes (iii) and (v) wrong.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-099","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":99,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about human oogenesis are correct?\n(i) Oogonia are formed within the fetal ovary during embryonic development.\n(ii) The primary oocyte completes its first meiotic division while inside the secondary follicle.\n(iii) The first meiotic division of the primary oocyte gives a large haploid secondary oocyte and a tiny first polar body.\n(iv) The zona pellucida is a new membrane formed by the secondary oocyte.\n(v) The cell released at ovulation has already completed the second meiotic division.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (iii) and (iv) are correct. Meiosis I is completed in the tertiary follicle, not the secondary follicle. The cell released at ovulation is a secondary oocyte, which completes meiosis II only when a sperm enters it.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-100","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":100,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In human males, the testes are situated outside the abdominal cavity in the scrotum.\nReason (R): Spermatogenesis requires a temperature lower than the normal internal body temperature.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The testes lie in the scrotum, outside the abdominal cavity. This position keeps them 2–2.5 °C cooler, which spermatogenesis needs, so R correctly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-101","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":101,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"During the luteal phase, which structure secretes large amounts of progesterone, and what is the main role of this hormone?","image":"","options":["Graafian follicle; induces ovulation","Anterior pituitary; stimulates follicular development","Corpus luteum; maintains the endometrium","Placenta; causes menstruation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"After ovulation, the remaining parts of the Graafian follicle form the corpus luteum. It secretes large amounts of progesterone, which maintains the endometrium needed for implantation. Ovulation itself is induced by the LH surge, not by progesterone.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-102","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":102,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Arrange the following events of human oogenesis in the correct chronological order:\nA. The primary oocyte becomes surrounded by a layer of granulosa cells.\nB. The Graafian follicle ruptures, releasing the secondary oocyte.\nC. Oogonia enter meiosis and are arrested in prophase-I.\nD. More granulosa layers and a new theca surround the follicle.\nE. An antrum appears, and the primary oocyte completes its first meiotic division.","image":"","options":["C, A, D, E, B","A, C, D, E, B","C, D, A, E, B","C, A, E, D, B"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Oogonia first become primary oocytes arrested in prophase-I (C). Each is then wrapped by granulosa cells to form a primary follicle (A), which becomes a secondary follicle with a theca (D). Next, the tertiary follicle forms an antrum and completes meiosis I (E), and finally the Graafian follicle ruptures at ovulation (B). The key trap is placing the completion of meiosis I before the theca stage.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-103","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":103,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"When the Graafian follicle ruptures at ovulation in a human female, the cell released from the ovary is the:","image":"","options":["Primary oocyte that is still arrested in prophase I","Ootid formed after the second meiotic division","Secondary oocyte, which has not yet completed meiosis II","Oogonium that is about to begin meiosis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Meiosis I is completed in the tertiary follicle, so the cell released at ovulation is a secondary oocyte. Meiosis II, which forms the ootid and second polar body, is completed only after a sperm enters. A common trap is to assume a fully mature ovum is released.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-104","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":104,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"A researcher examines a section of a testis. Type P cells sit in the lining of the seminiferous tubules alongside dividing germ cells. Type Q cells lie in clusters in the spaces between the tubules, near small blood vessels. A chemical selectively destroys only the type Q cells. Which effect would appear first?","image":"","options":["Germ cells inside the tubules lose their nutritional support","Secretion of androgens falls sharply","The testes can no longer be kept below body temperature","Spermatogonia disappear from the tubule lining immediately"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Type Q cells are Leydig cells in the interstitial spaces, and they secrete androgens, so androgen output falls first. Nutrition of germ cells depends on Sertoli cells (type P), which remain intact, and temperature control depends on the scrotum.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-105","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":105,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The signals that initiate parturition originate from the maternal pituitary gland.\nStatement II: The inner cell mass contains stem cells that have the potency to give rise to all tissues and organs.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect because parturition signals originate from the fully developed foetus and the placenta. The maternal pituitary responds to these signals by releasing oxytocin. Statement II is correct as stated in NCERT.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-106","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":106,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following shows the correct sequence of structures through which sperms pass from their site of formation to the outside?","image":"","options":["Seminiferous tubule → Vasa efferentia → Rete testis → Epididymis → Vas deferens → Ejaculatory duct → Urethra","Seminiferous tubule → Rete testis → Epididymis → Vasa efferentia → Vas deferens → Urethra → Ejaculatory duct","Seminiferous tubule → Rete testis → Vasa efferentia → Epididymis → Vas deferens → Ejaculatory duct → Urethra","Seminiferous tubule → Rete testis → Vasa efferentia → Vas deferens → Epididymis → Ejaculatory duct → Urethra"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Sperms move from the seminiferous tubules to the rete testis and then the vasa efferentia. From there they pass to the epididymis and the vas deferens. The vas deferens opens into the urethra as the ejaculatory duct, so the urethra comes last.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-107","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":107,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Besides contributing to the seminal plasma, the secretion of which male accessory glands also helps in lubrication of the penis?","image":"","options":["Seminal vesicles","Prostate","Bulbourethral glands","Leydig cells"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The bulbourethral glands are the accessory glands whose secretion also helps lubricate the penis. Leydig cells are not accessory glands; they are interstitial cells of the testis that secrete androgens.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-108","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":108,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"In the human testis, the seminiferous tubules open into the vasa efferentia through the:","image":"","options":["Epididymis","Rete testis","Ejaculatory duct","Vas deferens"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The seminiferous tubules first open into the rete testis, which then leads into the vasa efferentia. The epididymis and vas deferens come later in the duct pathway.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-109","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":109,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about gametogenesis in humans are correct?\n(i) Oogonia are formed in the fetal ovary, and no new oogonia are added after birth.\n(ii) Primary oocytes remain temporarily arrested in prophase-I of meiosis.\n(iii) The primary oocyte completes its first meiotic division while inside the primary follicle.\n(iv) Each secondary spermatocyte contains 23 chromosomes.\n(v) The secondary oocyte completes its second meiotic division before it is released at ovulation.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (ii) and (iv) are correct, so the count is 3. The first meiotic division of the oocyte is completed in the tertiary follicle, not the primary follicle. The second meiotic division is completed only after a sperm enters the oocyte, not before ovulation.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-110","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":110,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Absence of menstruation in a woman of reproductive age always indicates pregnancy.\nReason (R): During pregnancy, all events of the menstrual cycle stop and there is no menstruation.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"R is true: the menstrual cycle stops during pregnancy. A is false: NCERT notes that missed menstruation may also be caused by stress, poor health or other factors, so it only may indicate pregnancy.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-111","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":111,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Surgical removal of both testes in an adult male would also impair the normal functioning of his seminal vesicles and prostate.\nReason (R): The functions of the male sex accessory ducts and glands are maintained by androgens secreted by the Leydig cells of the testes.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The accessory ducts and glands depend on androgens, which are made by Leydig cells in the testes. Removing the testes removes this androgen source, so the seminal vesicles and prostate stop functioning normally. R therefore directly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-112","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":112,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(a) GnRH\n(b) LH\n(c) FSH\n(d) Androgens\nList-II\n(i) Acts on Sertoli cells to stimulate secretion of factors that help spermiogenesis\n(ii) Hypothalamic hormone acting on the anterior pituitary\n(iii) Stimulate the process of spermatogenesis\n(iv) Stimulates Leydig cells to synthesise androgens\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"GnRH comes from the hypothalamus and acts on the anterior pituitary. The pituitary then releases LH, which acts on Leydig cells, and FSH, which acts on Sertoli cells. The androgens made by Leydig cells stimulate spermatogenesis.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-113","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":113,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The presence or absence of the hymen is not a reliable indicator of virginity or sexual experience.\nReason (R): The hymen can be torn by a sudden fall or jolt, insertion of a tampon, or sports like cycling and horseback riding, and in some women it persists even after coitus.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The hymen can break without coitus and can remain intact after coitus. This is exactly why its state cannot indicate sexual experience, so R correctly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-114","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":114,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"The menstrual phase of the human menstrual cycle lasts about ____ days, and the menstrual flow results from ____.","image":"","options":["3–5; breakdown of the endometrial lining of the uterus and its blood vessels","3–5; breakdown of the myometrium of the uterus","10–12; rupture of the Graafian follicle","7–9; shedding of the cervical lining"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Menstrual flow lasts 3–5 days. It is caused by the breakdown of the endometrium and its blood vessels, which forms a liquid that leaves through the vagina. The myometrium is the muscular layer and is not shed.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-115","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":115,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"In the later phase of human pregnancy, the hormone relaxin is secreted by the:","image":"","options":["Placenta","Ovary","Anterior pituitary","Endometrium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that relaxin is secreted by the ovary in late pregnancy. A common trap is to list it among the placental hormones, which are hCG, hPL, estrogens and progestogens.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-116","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":116,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"During cleavage, the human embryo that consists of 8 to 16 blastomeres is called the:","image":"","options":["Blastocyst","Morula","Zygote","Trophoblast"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The embryo at the 8–16 blastomere stage is the morula. It keeps dividing and becomes the blastocyst as it moves further into the uterus.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-117","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":117,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"A woman has a regular 28-day menstrual cycle, and in this cycle the ovum is NOT fertilised. Starting just after the menstrual phase ends, arrange the following events in the correct order:\n(a) LH surge\n(b) Large-scale secretion of progesterone by the corpus luteum\n(c) Regeneration of the endometrium while follicles grow and secrete estrogens\n(d) Degeneration of the corpus luteum\n(e) Menstrual flow as the endometrium breaks down","image":"","options":["c → a → b → d → e","a → c → b → d → e","c → b → a → d → e","c → a → d → b → e"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In the follicular phase, growing follicles secrete estrogens and the endometrium regenerates. The mid-cycle LH surge then causes ovulation. In the luteal phase, the corpus luteum secretes progesterone. Without fertilisation, the corpus luteum degenerates, and the endometrium breaks down as menstruation. Progesterone secretion cannot come before ovulation, because the corpus luteum forms from the ruptured Graafian follicle.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-118","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":118,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following hormones is NOT produced in a woman exclusively during pregnancy?","image":"","options":["Human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG)","Human placental lactogen (hPL)","Relaxin","Prolactin"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT states that hCG, hPL and relaxin are produced only during pregnancy. Prolactin is present outside pregnancy too; its level merely increases several-fold during pregnancy.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-119","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":119,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about human sperm and semen are correct?\n(i) The acrosome is a cap-like structure over the anterior part of the haploid nucleus and is filled with enzymes that help in fertilisation.\n(ii) The mitochondria that power tail movement are located in the neck of the sperm.\n(iii) For normal fertility, at least 60 per cent of the ejaculated sperms must show vigorous motility.\n(iv) Secretions of the epididymis, vas deferens, seminal vesicle and prostate are essential for maturation and motility of sperms.\n(v) Androgens maintain the functions of the male sex accessory ducts and glands.\n(vi) The plasma membrane of a sperm covers only its head region.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (iv) and (v) are correct. The mitochondria lie in the middle piece, not the neck, and the plasma membrane envelops the whole sperm. The 60 per cent criterion refers to normal shape and size, whereas only 40 per cent of sperms need to show vigorous motility.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-120","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":120,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Normally only one sperm fertilises a human ovum, even though many sperms reach it.\nReason (R): Acrosomal secretions help the sperm pass through the zona pellucida and the plasma membrane into the cytoplasm of the ovum.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Both statements are true. Monospermy is ensured because contact of a sperm with the zona pellucida induces membrane changes that block the entry of additional sperms. The acrosomal enzymes only help sperm entry, so R does not explain A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-121","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":121,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Fimbriae help in collecting the ovum after ovulation.\nReason (R): Fimbriae are finger-like projections present at the edges of the isthmus.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Fimbriae do collect the ovum after ovulation, so A is true. However, they are located on the edges of the infundibulum, the part nearest the ovary, not on the isthmus, so R is false.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-122","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":122,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following cells are haploid?\nSpermatogonium, primary spermatocyte, secondary spermatocyte, spermatid, primary oocyte, first polar body, ootid, zygote","image":"","options":["3","5","4","2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The secondary spermatocyte, spermatid, first polar body and ootid are formed after the reduction division, so these 4 are haploid. Spermatogonium, primary spermatocyte, primary oocyte and zygote are diploid. The usual trap is forgetting that the first polar body is also haploid.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-123","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":123,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the human female reproductive system are correct?\n(i) Each ovary is about 2 to 4 cm long and is attached to the pelvic wall and uterus by ligaments.\n(ii) The ovarian stroma is enclosed by a thin epithelium and is differentiated into an outer medulla and an inner cortex.\n(iii) The isthmus is the wider part of the oviduct, and it opens into the ampulla.\n(iv) Each fallopian tube is about 10-12 cm long and extends from the periphery of the ovary to the uterus.\n(v) The uterus is single, shaped like an inverted pear, and supported by ligaments attached to the pelvic wall.\n(vi) The ovaries are primary sex organs that produce the ovum as well as several steroid hormones.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (iv), (v) and (vi) are correct, which makes four. Statement (ii) is wrong because the cortex is peripheral and the medulla is inner. Statement (iii) is wrong because the isthmus is the narrow last part of the oviduct that joins the uterus, while the ampulla is the wider part.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-124","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":124,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about a human sperm are correct?\n(i) The head contains an elongated haploid nucleus.\n(ii) The acrosome covers the posterior portion of the nucleus.\n(iii) The plasma membrane envelops only the head region.\n(iv) The middle piece possesses numerous mitochondria.\n(v) The sperm is composed of a head, neck, middle piece and tail.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (iv) and (v) are correct. Statement (ii) is wrong because the acrosome covers the anterior portion of the nucleus. Statement (iii) is wrong because the plasma membrane envelops the whole body of the sperm.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-125","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":125,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about human oogenesis are correct?\n(i) The primary oocyte completes its first meiotic division within the tertiary follicle.\n(ii) A primary follicle is surrounded by both granulosa cells and a theca layer.\n(iii) The secondary oocyte produced by meiosis I is haploid.\n(iv) Degeneration of follicles between birth and puberty reduces their number in the ovary.\n(v) The ootid is released from the ovary at ovulation.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (iii) and (iv) are correct. Statement (ii) is wrong because the theca first appears in the secondary follicle. Statement (v) is wrong because a secondary oocyte, not an ootid, is released; the ootid forms only after sperm entry.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-126","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":126,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Loss of androgen secretion in an adult male would impair the maturation and motility of sperms even after they have been released from the seminiferous tubules.\nReason (R): The functions of the male sex accessory ducts and glands, whose secretions are essential for sperm maturation and motility, are maintained by androgens.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Secretions of the epididymis, vas deferens, seminal vesicle and prostate are needed for sperm maturation and motility. Androgens maintain these organs, so losing androgens disrupts these post-release steps. R therefore directly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-127","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":127,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Mitotic divisions of the zygote, called cleavage, begin as it moves through the isthmus of the oviduct towards the uterus.\nStatement II: The embryo with 8 to 16 blastomeres is called a blastocyst.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Cleavage starts as the zygote passes through the isthmus. The 8–16 celled stage is the morula, not the blastocyst. The blastocyst forms later, as the morula moves further into the uterus.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-128","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":128,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The secondary oocyte completes its second meiotic division before it is released from the Graafian follicle.\nStatement II: The second meiotic division of the oocyte is unequal and produces a second polar body and a haploid ootid.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The ovum is released as a secondary oocyte. Its second meiotic division is completed only when a sperm enters it, so Statement I is wrong. That division is unequal and gives a second polar body and an ootid, so Statement II is correct.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-129","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":129,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Suppose 100 primary spermatocytes in a seminiferous tubule complete both meiotic divisions normally. How many secondary spermatocytes and how many spermatids, respectively, will be formed?","image":"","options":["100 and 200","200 and 400","200 and 200","400 and 400"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Each primary spermatocyte gives two secondary spermatocytes in meiosis I, so 100 give 200. Each secondary spermatocyte gives two spermatids in meiosis II, so 200 give 400. Unlike oogenesis, no polar bodies form, so all four products of each primary spermatocyte are functional.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-130","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":130,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"An experimental toxin selectively destroys only those cells in the testis that lie in the interstitial spaces and synthesise androgens. All other testicular cells remain intact. Which outcome is most likely?","image":"","options":["Androgen secretion falls and spermatogenesis is impaired, even though spermatogonia and Sertoli cells are still present inside the seminiferous tubules","Sertoli cells stop nourishing the germ cells, but androgen levels stay normal because spermatogonia take over androgen secretion","Androgen secretion is unaffected because Sertoli cells are the main source of testicular androgens","Spermatogenesis continues normally because it depends only on the lower scrotal temperature"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The destroyed cells are Leydig cells, which are the source of androgens, and androgens stimulate spermatogenesis. Losing them therefore impairs sperm production even though the cells lining the tubules survive. Sertoli cells provide nutrition, not androgens, and a low temperature alone is not sufficient for spermatogenesis.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-131","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":131,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"The scrotum helps maintain the temperature of the testes at a level that is","image":"","options":["about 2–2.5 °C higher than the normal internal body temperature","about 2–2.5 °C lower than the normal internal body temperature","about 5–6 °C lower than the normal internal body temperature","equal to the normal internal body temperature"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The scrotum keeps the testes about 2–2.5 °C below internal body temperature, which spermatogenesis requires. A higher or equal temperature would not support sperm production.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-132","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":132,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (part of sperm) with List-II (feature/function):\nList-I\n(A) Acrosome\n(B) Middle piece\n(C) Tail\n(D) Plasma membrane\nList-II\n(I) Envelops the whole body of the sperm\n(II) Contains enzymes that help fertilisation\n(III) Its movement gives the sperm motility\n(IV) Contains numerous mitochondria\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The acrosome carries enzymes (II), and the middle piece carries mitochondria (IV). The tail's movement provides motility (III), and the plasma membrane covers the entire sperm (I).","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-133","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":133,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Scientifically, the sex of a human baby is determined by the father and not by the mother.\nReason (R): All ova carry an X chromosome, whereas half of the sperms carry an X chromosome and half carry a Y chromosome.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Since every ovum contributes an X, whether the zygote is XX (female) or XY (male) depends only on the sperm. R therefore directly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-134","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":134,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (time during human pregnancy) with List-II (developmental feature):\nList-I\nA. After one month\nB. By the end of second month\nC. During the fifth month\nD. By the end of 24 weeks\nList-II\nI. Limbs and digits develop\nII. Eyelids separate and eyelashes are formed\nIII. Heart is formed\nIV. First foetal movements and hair on the head appear\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The sequence runs: heart at one month, limbs and digits by month two, first movements and head hair in month five, and separated eyelids with eyelashes by 24 weeks (end of the second trimester).","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-135","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":135,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"The gamete mother cells formed in large numbers within each fetal ovary during embryonic development are called:","image":"","options":["Primary follicles","Granulosa cells","Ootids","Oogonia"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A couple of million oogonia (gamete mother cells) form in each fetal ovary, and none are added after birth. Primary follicles appear only later, when primary oocytes become surrounded by granulosa cells.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-136","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":136,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following structures are parts of the female external genitalia?\nMons pubis, labia majora, cervix, clitoris, fimbriae, hymen, labia minora, isthmus","image":"","options":["4","5","6","7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The female external genitalia are the mons pubis, labia majora, labia minora, hymen and clitoris, which makes five. The cervix belongs to the uterus, while the fimbriae and isthmus are parts of the oviduct.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-137","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":137,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following cells are haploid?\nSpermatogonium, primary spermatocyte, secondary spermatocyte, spermatid, spermatozoon","image":"","options":["Two","Four","Three","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Secondary spermatocytes, spermatids and spermatozoa are haploid (23 chromosomes), so the count is three. The trap is the primary spermatocyte: it is still diploid (46), because reduction happens only when it completes meiosis I.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-138","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":138,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following features are typically achieved by the end of the first trimester (12 weeks) of human pregnancy?\n(i) Most of the major organ systems are formed\n(ii) Limbs are well developed\n(iii) External genital organs are well developed\n(iv) Eyelashes are formed\n(v) Body is covered with fine hair","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"By 12 weeks, most major organ systems have formed, and the limbs and external genitalia are well developed. That makes three features. Fine body hair and eyelashes appear only by the end of about 24 weeks.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-139","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":139,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: After spermiogenesis, the heads of the sperms become embedded in the Sertoli cells.\nStatement II: The release of sperms from the seminiferous tubules is called spermiogenesis.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"After spermiogenesis, sperm heads do lie embedded in Sertoli cells, so Statement I is correct. Their release from the seminiferous tubules is called spermiation, not spermiogenesis, so Statement II is incorrect.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-140","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":140,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"In a woman whose released ovum has not been fertilised, the corpus luteum is (hypothetically) removed early in the luteal phase. Based on the hormonal control of the menstrual cycle, which outcome is expected?","image":"","options":["Progesterone falls, the endometrium disintegrates and menstruation begins, marking a new cycle","The endometrium thickens further because estrogen secretion by the corpus luteum stops","A second ovum is immediately released from the same Graafian follicle","Menstruation stops, just as it does during pregnancy"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The endometrium is maintained by progesterone from the corpus luteum. Removing it mimics natural degeneration of the corpus luteum when there is no fertilisation, so the endometrium breaks down and menstruation follows. Menstruation stops only during pregnancy, when the endometrium is maintained.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-141","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":141,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Luteinising hormone (LH) stimulates the synthesis and secretion of androgens in the human male by acting on which cells?","image":"","options":["Sertoli cells","Spermatogonia","Leydig cells","Primary spermatocytes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"LH acts on the Leydig (interstitial) cells, which lie outside the seminiferous tubules and secrete androgens. FSH, not LH, acts on Sertoli cells.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-142","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":142,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Arrange the following events of oogenesis and follicle development in the correct chronological order:\n(a) Formation of zona pellucida around the secondary oocyte\n(b) Appearance of a fluid-filled antrum\n(c) Completion of the first meiotic division with release of the first polar body\n(d) Primary oocyte becomes surrounded by a layer of granulosa cells\n(e) Addition of more granulosa layers and a new theca","image":"","options":["(d) → (b) → (e) → (c) → (a)","(d) → (e) → (c) → (b) → (a)","(e) → (d) → (b) → (a) → (c)","(d) → (e) → (b) → (c) → (a)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The sequence is: primary follicle forms (d), then the secondary follicle gains more granulosa layers and a theca (e). Next the tertiary follicle develops an antrum (b), and within it the primary oocyte completes meiosis I (c). Finally, in the Graafian follicle, the secondary oocyte forms the zona pellucida (a).","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-143","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":143,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Arrange the following events in the correct order, starting just after fertilisation:\n(a) Uterine cells divide and cover the blastocyst\n(b) Formation of an 8–16 celled embryo\n(c) Appearance of chorionic villi on the trophoblast\n(d) Attachment of trophoblast to the endometrium\n(e) Differentiation of blastomeres into trophoblast and inner cell mass","image":"","options":["b, e, d, a, c","b, d, e, a, c","e, b, d, c, a","b, e, a, d, c"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The morula forms first and then becomes a blastocyst with trophoblast and inner cell mass. The trophoblast attaches to the endometrium, uterine cells cover the blastocyst (implantation), and only after implantation do chorionic villi appear.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-144","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":144,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"The ovarian stroma of a human ovary is divided into:","image":"","options":["An outer medulla and an inner cortex","A peripheral cortex and an inner medulla","Theca interna and theca externa","Perimetrium and endometrium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The ovarian stroma is enclosed by a thin epithelium and has a peripheral cortex and an inner medulla. Theca interna and theca externa are layers of a follicle, not zones of the stroma.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-145","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":145,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are found in the interstitial spaces of the human testis?\nLeydig cells, small blood vessels, Sertoli cells, spermatogonia, immunologically competent cells","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Leydig cells, small blood vessels and immunologically competent cells occur in the interstitial spaces. Sertoli cells and spermatogonia line the inside of the seminiferous tubules.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-146","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":146,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"The scrotum keeps the testes at a temperature lower than the normal internal body temperature, which is needed for spermatogenesis. By how much is the testicular temperature lowered?","image":"","options":["0.5–1 °C","2–2.5 °C","4–5 °C","6–8 °C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the scrotum keeps the testes 2–2.5 °C below the internal body temperature, which is necessary for sperm formation. The option 4–5 °C uses the number for testis length (in cm), which is a common trap.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-147","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":147,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"During human pregnancy, by the end of which period are the limbs and digits of the foetus first developed?","image":"","options":["End of the first month","End of the second month","End of the fifth month","End of 24 weeks"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Limbs and digits develop by the end of the second month. The trap is the first month, which is when the heart forms. The fifth month brings the first movements and head hair, and 24 weeks brings separated eyelids and eyelashes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-148","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":148,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"The mitotic division of the zygote, called cleavage, starts as the zygote moves towards the uterus through which part of the oviduct?","image":"","options":["Infundibulum","Fimbriae","Isthmus","Cervix"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Fertilisation takes place in the ampulla. Cleavage then begins as the zygote travels through the isthmus towards the uterus.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-149","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":149,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Spermatogonia on the inside wall of seminiferous tubules increase in number by mitotic division.\nReason (R): Each spermatogonium is diploid and contains 46 chromosomes.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Both statements are true: spermatogonia multiply by mitosis, and they are diploid with 46 chromosomes. However, being diploid does not explain why they divide by mitosis to increase in number, so R is not the explanation of A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-150","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":150,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"A hypothetical drug selectively blocks LH receptors on Leydig cells but does not affect FSH receptors. Which outcome is most likely in an adult male?","image":"","options":["Androgen secretion falls and spermatogenesis is impaired, even though FSH can still act on Sertoli cells","Sertoli cells stop responding to FSH, so spermiogenesis stops, but androgen levels remain normal","GnRH secretion from the anterior pituitary stops completely","Only spermiation is blocked, while androgen secretion and meiosis continue normally"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"LH acts on Leydig cells to make androgens, and androgens stimulate spermatogenesis. Blocking LH receptors therefore lowers androgens and impairs sperm formation, even though the FSH–Sertoli pathway is untouched. GnRH is a hypothalamic hormone, not a pituitary one.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-151","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":151,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Arrange the following events of human parturition in the correct sequence:\nA. Release of oxytocin from the maternal pituitary\nB. Signals from the fully developed foetus and placenta\nC. Expulsion of the placenta\nD. Mild uterine contractions (foetal ejection reflex)\nE. Stronger uterine contractions and expulsion of the baby through the birth canal","image":"","options":["B → D → A → E → C","D → B → A → E → C","B → A → D → C → E","A → B → D → E → C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Signals from the foetus and placenta induce mild contractions, the foetal ejection reflex. This triggers oxytocin release, which causes stronger contractions that expel the baby. The placenta is expelled only after the infant is delivered.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-152","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":152,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"The foetal ejection reflex triggers the release of which hormone from the maternal pituitary?","image":"","options":["Prolactin","Oxytocin","Relaxin","Human chorionic gonadotropin"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Mild uterine contractions (the foetal ejection reflex) trigger oxytocin release from the maternal pituitary. Relaxin comes from the ovary, and hCG is a placental hormone.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-153","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":153,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"In the Graafian follicle, the secondary oocyte forms a new membrane around itself. This membrane is the:","image":"","options":["Theca externa","Zona pellucida","Theca interna","Germinal epithelium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The secondary oocyte in the Graafian follicle forms a new membrane called the zona pellucida. A sperm later contacts this layer during fertilisation. The theca layers belong to the follicle wall and are not secreted by the oocyte.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-154","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":154,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (stage of ovarian follicle) with List-II (characteristic feature).\nList-I\nA. Primary follicle\nB. Secondary follicle\nC. Tertiary follicle\nD. Graafian follicle\nList-II\nI. Ruptures to release the secondary oocyte\nII. Primary oocyte surrounded by a single layer of granulosa cells\nIII. Theca differentiates into theca interna and theca externa\nIV. More layers of granulosa cells and a new theca\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A primary follicle has one layer of granulosa cells. The secondary follicle gains more granulosa layers and a new theca. In the tertiary follicle, the theca is organised into interna and externa, and the Graafian follicle ruptures at ovulation. The common trap is thinking the theca splits into two layers at the secondary stage.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-155","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":155,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Implantation is complete when, after the trophoblast attaches, uterine cells divide rapidly and cover the blastocyst, embedding it in the endometrium.\nStatement II: The placenta is formed entirely from embryonic tissue, without any contribution from maternal uterine tissue.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I correctly describes implantation. Statement II is wrong because the placenta forms jointly from chorionic villi and uterine tissue that interdigitate with each other.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-156","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":156,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"The glandular tissue of each human breast is divided into how many mammary lobes?","image":"","options":["5–10","15–20","25–30","About 250"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Each breast has 15–20 mammary lobes, and each lobe contains clusters of alveoli. About 250 is the number of testicular lobules in a testis, which is a common mix-up.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-157","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":157,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"In a man, the vas deferens on both sides is cut and tied just beyond the epididymis. All other ducts and glands remain normal. What would the ejaculated fluid most likely be like?","image":"","options":["It would contain sperms but no fructose","It would lack sperms but still contain seminal plasma, including fructose-rich secretions","It would contain neither sperms nor any glandular secretions","It would contain sperms and seminal plasma in normal amounts"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The duct from the seminal vesicle joins the vas deferens beyond the cut to form the ejaculatory duct. The prostate and bulbourethral glands also open beyond the cut. So seminal plasma still reaches the urethra, but sperms from the testis cannot.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-158","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":158,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"The cap-like structure covering the anterior portion of the sperm nucleus is filled with enzymes. What is the main role of this structure?","image":"","options":["Producing energy for the movement of the tail","Helping the sperm fertilise the ovum","Storing nutrients for the developing zygote","Protecting the haploid nucleus from mechanical damage"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The acrosome contains enzymes. Its secretions help the sperm pass through the zona pellucida and plasma membrane into the ovum cytoplasm. Energy for movement comes from the middle piece, not the acrosome.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-159","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":159,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"In a diagram of a human sperm, one region lies between the neck and the tail and is packed with numerous mitochondria. What is the function of this region?","image":"","options":["It contains enzymes that help the sperm penetrate the ovum","It houses the haploid nucleus carrying paternal chromosomes","It produces energy for the movement of the tail","It secretes fructose for the nourishment of the sperm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The middle piece has many mitochondria, which supply energy for tail movement and sperm motility. Penetration enzymes are in the acrosome, and fructose comes from the seminal plasma, not from the sperm itself.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-160","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":160,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about human pregnancy is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Chorionic villi are surrounded by uterine tissue and maternal blood","The placenta helps remove carbon dioxide and excretory wastes produced by the embryo","Levels of cortisol and thyroxine in maternal blood fall during pregnancy to lower the mother's metabolic load","The inner cell mass contains stem cells that can give rise to all tissues and organs"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"During pregnancy, cortisol, thyroxine, prolactin, estrogens and progestogens all rise several-fold in maternal blood. These raised levels support fetal growth and the metabolic changes in the mother, so the claim that they fall is incorrect.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-161","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":161,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): hCG, hPL and relaxin are produced in women only during pregnancy.\nReason (R): All three of these hormones are secreted by the placenta.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"All three hormones are indeed produced only during pregnancy, so A is true. R is false because relaxin comes from the ovary, while hCG and hPL come from the placenta.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-162","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":162,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Approximately how many testicular lobules are present in each human testis?","image":"","options":["About 25","About 100","About 250","About 2500"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Each testis is divided into about 250 compartments called testicular lobules.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-163","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":163,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Not every act of copulation results in fertilisation and pregnancy.\nReason (R): Fertilisation can occur only if the ovum and the sperms are transported to the ampullary region at the same time.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT states that fertilisation requires the ovum and sperms to reach the ampullary region simultaneously. This timing requirement is exactly why many copulations do not lead to pregnancy, so R explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-164","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":164,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"A healthy woman has a regular 28-day menstrual cycle, and day 1 is the first day of menstrual flow. A diagram of her ovarian and uterine events is examined at day 21 of a cycle in which fertilisation has not occurred. Which description best fits day 21?","image":"","options":["The Graafian follicle is about to rupture under the influence of the LH surge","A corpus luteum is present and secreting large amounts of progesterone, which maintains the endometrium","The endometrium is breaking down and menstrual flow is occurring","Primary follicles are maturing under gradually rising LH and FSH while the endometrium begins to regenerate"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Ovulation occurs around day 14, so day 21 lies in the luteal phase. Here the corpus luteum secretes progesterone that maintains the endometrium. The other options describe ovulation, the menstrual phase and the follicular phase.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-165","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":165,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Arrange the following events of human fertilisation in the correct sequence:\nP. Fusion of the haploid nuclei of the sperm and the ovum\nQ. Sperm comes in contact with the zona pellucida, inducing changes that block other sperms\nR. Formation of the second polar body and the ootid\nS. Acrosomal secretions help the sperm enter the cytoplasm of the ovum","image":"","options":["S → Q → P → R","Q → R → S → P","Q → S → R → P","S → R → Q → P"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The events occur in this order:\n- The sperm first contacts the zona pellucida (Q).\n- Acrosomal secretions then let it enter the ovum (S).\n- Its entry triggers completion of meiosis II, giving the second polar body and the ootid (R).\n- Only after that do the two haploid nuclei fuse (P).\nNuclear fusion cannot come before meiosis II is completed.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-166","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":166,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the human male reproductive system is incorrect?","image":"","options":["The vas deferens ascends to the abdomen and loops over the urinary bladder","The urethra originates from the urinary bladder and extends through the penis","The prostate is a paired accessory gland like the seminal vesicles","The male accessory ducts store and transport sperms from the testis to the outside"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The prostate is a single gland, whereas the seminal vesicles and bulbourethral glands are paired. The other three statements correctly describe the vas deferens, the urethra and the role of the ducts.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-167","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":167,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following structures of the human male reproductive system are paired?\nSeminal vesicles, Prostate, Bulbourethral glands, Urethra, Testes","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The seminal vesicles, bulbourethral glands and testes are paired, which gives three. The prostate is a single gland and the urethra is a single tube; assuming the prostate is paired is the common trap.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-168","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":168,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"A sperm has a normal nucleus, middle piece and tail, but completely lacks an acrosome. Which outcome is most likely if it reaches an ovum in the ampulla?","image":"","options":["It cannot swim, because the acrosome supplies energy to the tail","It can reach the ovum but cannot enter its cytoplasm through the zona pellucida and plasma membrane","It enters the ovum but cannot fuse with its nucleus, since the acrosome contains the sperm's chromosomes","It enters the ovum normally, because penetration depends only on mitochondrial energy"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Motility depends on the middle piece mitochondria and the tail, both intact here, so this sperm can still swim to the ovum. Entry into the ovum depends on acrosomal secretions, so without an acrosome it cannot penetrate the zona pellucida and plasma membrane. The chromosomes lie in the nucleus, not in the acrosome.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-169","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":169,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Trophoblast\n(B) Inner cell mass\n(C) Chorionic villi\n(D) Umbilical cord\nList-II\n(I) Connects placenta to the embryo\n(II) Interdigitates with uterine tissue to form placenta\n(III) Differentiates into the embryo\n(IV) Attaches to the endometrium","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The trophoblast attaches to the endometrium, and the inner cell mass becomes the embryo. Chorionic villi interdigitate with uterine tissue to form the placenta, and the umbilical cord links the placenta to the embryo.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-170","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":170,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I (Follicle)\nA. Primary follicle\nB. Secondary follicle\nC. Tertiary follicle\nD. Graafian follicle\nList-II (Feature)\nI. Antrum present; theca organised into interna and externa\nII. Ruptures to release the secondary oocyte\nIII. Primary oocyte surrounded by a single layer of granulosa cells\nIV. More layers of granulosa cells and a new theca\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The primary follicle has one layer of granulosa cells. The secondary follicle gains more granulosa layers and a theca, and the tertiary follicle develops an antrum with theca interna and externa. The mature Graafian follicle ruptures at ovulation.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-171","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":171,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): At puberty, each human ovary contains only about 60,000–80,000 primary follicles, although a couple of million oogonia were formed in each fetal ovary.\nReason (R): No new oogonia are formed after birth, and a large number of follicles degenerate between birth and puberty.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Oogonia are produced only during fetal life, and many primary follicles degenerate from birth to puberty. Together, these two facts explain why only 60,000–80,000 follicles remain in each ovary at puberty.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-172","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":172,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Sertoli cells\n(B) Leydig cells\n(C) Male germ cells (spermatogonia)\n(D) Scrotum\nList-II\n(i) Undergo meiotic divisions that finally lead to sperm formation\n(ii) Maintain the testes at a temperature lower than body temperature\n(iii) Provide nutrition to germ cells\n(iv) Secrete androgens\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Sertoli cells nourish the germ cells, Leydig cells secrete androgens, germ cells undergo meiosis to form sperms, and the scrotum keeps the testes cooler. The usual trap is swapping the roles of Sertoli and Leydig cells.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-HUMAN-REPRO-173","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Human Reproduction","qno":173,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Human Reproduction","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the follicular phase of the human menstrual cycle are correct?\n(i) Primary follicles grow to become a fully mature Graafian follicle.\n(ii) The endometrium of the uterus regenerates through proliferation.\n(iii) Secretion of LH and FSH gradually decreases.\n(iv) The growing follicles secrete estrogens.\n(v) The corpus luteum secretes large amounts of progesterone.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (ii) and (iv) are correct. LH and FSH rise gradually during this phase rather than fall. The corpus luteum forms only after ovulation, in the luteal phase.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 2 · Human Reproduction","difficulty":"medium"}]});
